&amp;usg=3DAOvVaw2IHBtXFUEnujdan6MyqcfJ">Replication Layer (cockroachlabs.c=
om)</a></span></li></ul><h1 class=3D"c47" id=3D"h.4em8j6ci58qo"><span class=
=3D"c44 c6">Introduction</span></h1><p class=3D"c13"><span>This assignment =
focuses on implementing</span><span class=3D"c1">&nbsp;a modified Raft syst=
em similar to those used by geo-distributed Database clusters such as Cockr=
oachDB or YugabyteDB. Raft is a consensus algorithm designed for distribute=
d systems to ensure fault tolerance and consistency. It operates through le=
ader election, log replication, and commitment of entries across a cluster =
of nodes.</span></p><p class=3D"c9"><span class=3D"c1"></span></p><p class=
=3D"c12"><span>We aim to build a database that stores key-value pairs </spa=
n><span>mapping string (key) to string (value)</span><span>. The Raft clust=
er maintains this database and ensures fault tolerance and </span><span cla=
ss=3D"c5"><a class=3D"c4" href=3D"https://www.google.com/url?q=3Dhttps://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strong_consistency&amp;sa=3DD&amp;source=3Deditors&amp;=
ust=3D1710480778646049&amp;usg=3DAOvVaw351faHKn_G8zA41fDiH-6q">strong </a><=
/span><span class=3D"c5"><a class=3D"c4" href=3D"https://www.google.com/url=
?q=3Dhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strong_consistency&amp;sa=3DD&amp;source=
=3Deditors&amp;ust=3D1710480778646131&amp;usg=3DAOvVaw1pW_7WMrxzq1P3rojStJJ=
k">consistency</a></span><span>. The client requests the server to perform =
operations on this database reliably.</span></p><hr><p class=3D"c13 c40 c22=
"><span class=3D"c1"></span></p><h1 class=3D"c47" id=3D"h.8phkv1azdzj7"><sp=
an class=3D"c44 c6">Raft Modification (for faster Reads)</span></h1><p clas=
s=3D"c13"><span>Traditionally, Raft requires the leader to exchange a heart=
beat with a majority of peers before responding to a read request (</span><=
span class=3D"c5"><a class=3D"c4" href=3D"https://www.google.com/url?q=3Dht=
tps://www.ditsing.com/read-only-requests-in-raft-part-1/&amp;sa=3DD&amp;sou=
rce=3Deditors&amp;ust=3D1710480778646456&amp;usg=3DAOvVaw1pjvLKcLmB-Psj6J70=
zXCZ">How Raft handles read requests</a></span><span>).</span><span class=
=3D"c1">&nbsp;If there are n nodes in the cluster, each read operation cost=
s O(n). By requiring these heartbeats, Raft introduces a network hop betwee=
n peers in a read operation, which can result in high read latencies. The l=
atencies get much worse in the case of multi-region geo-distributed databas=
es where the nodes are located physically far apart.</span></p><p class=3D"=
c13 c22"><span class=3D"c1"></span></p><h2 class=3D"c24" id=3D"h.9tamwyws8l=
kb"><span class=3D"c29 c6">Leader Lease</span></h2><p class=3D"c13"><span c=
lass=3D"c1">It is possible to rely on a time-based =E2=80=9Clease=E2=80=9D =
for Raft leadership that gets propagated through the heartbeat mechanism. A=
nd if we have well-behaved clocks, we can obtain linearizable reads without=
 paying a round-trip latency penalty. This is achieved using the concept of=
 Leases.</span></p><p class=3D"c13"><span class=3D"c5 c6"><a class=3D"c4" h=
ref=3D"https://www.google.com/url?q=3Dhttps://www.yugabyte.com/blog/low-lat=
ency-reads-in-geo-distributed-sql-with-raft-leader-leases/%23:~:text%3DThis=
%2520is%2520shown%2520in%2520the%2520animation%2520sequence%2520below.&amp;=
sa=3DD&amp;source=3Deditors&amp;ust=3D1710480778646949&amp;usg=3DAOvVaw0yHe=
ZJ7pKSAiIEmAtAXAvm">This animation</a></span><span class=3D"c1">&nbsp;shows=
 how leader leases work.</span></p><p class=3D"c13"><span class=3D"c36">Wha=
t is a Leader Lease?</span></p><p class=3D"c13"><span>Leases can be conside=
red as tokens that are valid for a certain period of time, known as </span>=
<span class=3D"c60">lease duration</span><span class=3D"c1">. A node can se=
rve read and write requests from the client if and only if the node has thi=
s valid token/lease.</span></p><p class=3D"c13"><span class=3D"c1">In Raft,=
 it is possible for more than one leader to exist (which is why even read r=
equests require a quorum to fetch the latest value), but leases are designe=
d in such a way that, at a time, only one lease can exist. Only leader node=
s are allowed to acquire a lease; therefore, the lease is known as the lead=
er lease.</span></p><p class=3D"c13 c22"><span class=3D"c1"></span></p><p c=
lass=3D"c13"><span class=3D"c36">Behavior of Leader &amp; Follower</span></=
p><p class=3D"c13"><span class=3D"c1">The leader acquires and renews the le=
ase using its heartbeat mechanism. When the leader has acquired/renewed its=
 lease, it starts a countdown (lease duration). If the leader is unable to =
renew its lease within this countdown, it needs to step down from being the=
 leader. </span></p><p class=3D"c13"><span class=3D"c1">The leader also pro=
pagates the end time of the acquired lease in its heartbeat. All the follow=
er nodes keep track of this leader lease timeout and use this information i=
n the next election process. </span></p><p class=3D"c13 c22"><span class=3D=
"c1"></span></p><p class=3D"c13"><span class=3D"c36">Leader Election</span>=
</p><p class=3D"c13"><span>During a leader election, a voter must propagate=
 the old leader=E2=80=99s lease timeout known to that voter to the new cand=
idate it is voting for. Upon receiving a majority of votes, the new leader =
must wait out the longest old leader=E2=80=99s lease duration before acquir=
ing its lease</span><span class=3D"c1">. The old leader steps down and no l=
onger functions as a leader upon the expiry of its leader lease.</span></p>=
<p class=3D"c13 c22"><span class=3D"c1"></span></p><hr><p class=3D"c13 c22 =
c40"><span class=3D"c1"></span></p><h1 class=3D"c47" id=3D"h.plmdnygfyhpr">=
<span class=3D"c44 c6">Implementation Details</span></h1><h2 class=3D"c30" =
id=3D"h.rsnypq80alac"><span class=3D"c6">0. </span><span class=3D"c29 c6">O=
verview</span></h2><p class=3D"c13"><span>In this assignment, you must impl=
ement the Raft algorithm with the leader lease modification. Each node will=
 be a process hosted on a separate Virtual Machine on Google Cloud, and the=
 client can reside either in Google Cloud=E2=80=99s Virtual Machine or in t=
he </span><span>local machine</span><span>. You can use </span><span class=
=3D"c20 c6">gRPC</span><span>&nbsp;(RECOMMENDED) or </span><span class=3D"c=
20 c6">ZeroMQ</span><span>&nbsp;for communication between nodes along with =
client-node interaction </span><span class=3D"c6">[NO other communication l=
ibrary is allowed]</span><span class=3D"c1">. </span></p><p class=3D"c13"><=
span>Changes related to leader lease have been highlighted in </span><span =
class=3D"c20">cyan</span><span class=3D"c1">.</span></p><p class=3D"c13 c22=
"><span class=3D"c1"></span></p><h2 class=3D"c30" id=3D"h.qqn1a8hx4x2t"><sp=
an class=3D"c6">1. Pseudo Code</span></h2><p class=3D"c17"><span>Many edge =
cases need to be handled while implementing the Raft algorithm. To ensure t=
hat all those edge cases have been handled correctly, you need to refer to =
the </span><span class=3D"c5 c6"><a class=3D"c4" href=3D"https://www.google=
.com/url?q=3Dhttps://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/teaching/2122/ConcDisSys/dist-sys-not=
es.pdf&amp;sa=3DD&amp;source=3Deditors&amp;ust=3D1710480778648587&amp;usg=
=3DAOvVaw1OM2y_kCLSTDW9X9zNEsxc">pseudo code</a></span><span>&nbsp;(pg 60 t=
